{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md | sed -n '540,735p'\"","aggregated_output":"   540\t### 5.1 Continuation transport across VM models\n   541\t\n   542\tA parked continuation resumes only under the VM model, and the image\n   543\tidentity, that parked it. This is a decided rule for B4 and R4, not a\n   544\tgap. Every parked record and reified continuation of either VM carries\n   545\tits model and image identity in its register payload, `{:format\n   546\t:yin.debruijn.code :hash H}` or `{:format :yin.debruijn.register :hash R}`\n   547\tbeside the fields the engine seam already lists, and a restore whose VM or\n   548\timage does not match refuses with a qualified `:continuation-format`\n   549\toutcome instead of interpreting a foreign payload. The engine never reads\n   550\tthose keys; they are register payload under the section 3 rule of\n   551\t`yin.vm.engine.md`.\n   552\t\n   553\tDirect cross-model resume is not possible, and a pairwise lift is the\n   554\twrong shape. A register park site and a stack park site for the same\n   555\tprogram point have no pc correspondence, a register file is not an\n   556\toperand stack, and each is a positional artifact of one lowering.\n   557\tTranslating one bytecode continuation into another is decompilation, and\n   558\twith a third model it becomes six lifts. This project already rules on\n   559\tthe shape: `docs/agents/architecture.md`, \"AGENTS\", Continuation\n   560\tMigration says the AST datoms are the canonical payload, bytecode is a\n   561\tprojection for one execution model, and a destination projects into\n   562\twhatever model it prefers. The cross-model form of a continuation is\n   563\ttherefore a continuation over the source, not over either image: pending\n   564\tframes named by resolved-tuple occurrence, plus the values those frames\n   565\thold. Both images carry provenance to source occurrences through their pc\n   566\tside tables, so each VM can supply a lift-out from its own continuation\n   567\tto that form and a lift-in from it, and no VM needs to know another\n   568\texists. That form is the universal continuation format the stack design\n   569\tnames as proposed and deferred. It is a shared concern of every VM, not\n   570\teither de Bruijn VM's, and belongs in its own design document; this\n   571\tdocument does not specify it.\n   572\t\n   573\tTwo consequences hold now. First, the section 4.5 live sets lose nothing\n   574\tobservable at that boundary: a register continuation carries only live\n   575\tregisters, and a dead register is by definition never read again, so a\n   576\tlift-out has every value any pending frame can observe. What does not\n   577\tsurvive a round trip is pc identity, temporary ids, and dead values, all\n   578\tof which the stack design's section 1 already declines to promise.\n   579\tSecond, until that format exists, cross-host transport of a parked\n   580\tcontinuation is same-model only: a register continuation travels to a\n   581\thost with a register kernel, a stack continuation to a host with the\n   582\tstack VM, and each such transfer also needs the image by R or H through\n   583\tthe R5 or B6 linker. This rule is deliberate; the alternative was a pair\n   584\tof lossy lifts between two positional formats that the third VM would\n   585\thave made obsolete.\n   586\t\n   587\t## 6. Implementation phases\n   588\t\n   589\tEach phase has a bounded file box, must-not-change list, completion criteria,\n   590\tand JVM, CLJS, CLJD, kondo, and cljstyle verification.\n   591\t\n   592\t### R0: contract and corpus\n   593\t\n   594\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_contract_test.cljc\n   595\t    Existing edits: none\n   596\t    Must not change: B0-B3, named datoms, stack image, resolver contract,\n   597\t    projection namespace\n   598\t\n   599\tThe resolver (`yin.vm.debruijn-resolve`: resolved tuples, side table,\n   600\tscope validation, the inverse used by tests) is now B2's own file box, per\n   601\tthe stack design's B2 phase box (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` section 3.1) --\n   602\tnot built here. R0 depends on it and freezes the register descriptor\n   603\tversion, operand mapping, normalizer fixtures, and the named-root corpus\n   604\tagainst it. Completion requires every node type from the resolver either\n   605\tmapped or refused with a named diagnostic, the duplicate-parameter and\n   606\tfree-name fixtures from B2, the section 2.2 address law between B2's stack\n   607\timages and the resolved tuples over the B0 parity corpus, pure data\n   608\tchecks, and a decision that no register kernel is assumed yet.\n   609\t\n   610\t### R1: register dimension and lowerer\n   611\t\n   612\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc\n   613\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc\n   614\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc\n   615\t    Existing edits: none\n   616\t    Must not change: B1 code, B2 lowerer, B3 VM, resolver contract,\n   617\t    dao.stream protocols\n   618\t\n   619\tImplement the descriptor, validation, deterministic lowering from resolved\n   620\trecords, allocation, encoding, R, and the lift to `:yin.code/*`. Completion\n   621\trequires every resolved node type mapped or refused with a named diagnostic,\n   622\tbyte-identical repeated lowerings, golden register vectors, cross-host R\n   623\tagreement, the section 2.2 address law extended to the register image, and\n   624\tthe lift law `lift(lower-register (resolve x), side-table)` alpha-equivalent\n   625\tto `canonical-vector(lower x)` under the arity and layout caveats of the\n   626\tstack design's B2 box.\n   627\t\n   628\t### R2: effects and stream forms\n   629\t\n   630\t    Existing source: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc\n   631\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects_test.cljc\n   632\t    Existing edits: none\n   633\t    Must not change: dao.stream, lease, waitset, B0-B3 semantics\n   634\t\n   635\tLower stream, gensym, FFI, park, and resume shapes with explicit registers.\n   636\tCompletion compares effect descriptors and blocked outcomes with the stack\n   637\tpath without executing a register VM.\n   638\t\n   639\t### R3: benchmark report\n   640\t\n   641\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_benchmark_test.cljc\n   642\t    Existing edits: none\n   643\t    Must not change: stack VM and its H\n   644\t\n   645\tInformational only, and sequenced after R4: it runs identical pure-program\n   646\tcorpora through the stack VM and the real R4 kernel, never a stand-in\n   647\tinterpreter, which the old gate needed only because the kernel was not yet\n   648\tauthorized. Report throughput, allocation, image size, load time, and\n   649\tlowering cost. This is the same role the stack design gives its own B3\n   650\treport: numbers the owner reads, not a condition any phase satisfies. It\n   651\tgates nothing, and no threshold is defined for it.\n   652\t\n   653\t### R4: register kernel\n   654\t\n   655\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc\n   656\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn/register_test.cljc\n   657\t    Existing edits: none\n   658\t    Must not change: B0-B3, named VM, existing IVM methods\n   659\t\n   660\tAuthorized unconditionally (section 8, DECIDED 1). Its prerequisite is R1:\n   661\ta validated register image and its R. R4 follows B3's own precedent and\n   662\tships in two tiers. The pure-program tier depends on R1 only: frames,\n   663\tclosures, loads, calls, returns, branches, constants, and store operations,\n   664\twith every opcode outside that set refused loudly with a\n   665\t`:not-yet-implemented` diagnostic, exactly as B3 does before B4. Its\n   666\tcompletion requires B0-normalized parity against the named VM and the stack\n   667\tVM over the pure-program corpus, all register validator fixtures, lexical\n   668\tand closure tests, and store and control flow tests. The effects tier\n   669\tdepends on R2, for the register shapes of stream, gensym, FFI, park, and\n   670\tresume, and on B4's engine seam, which supplies the restore and park-entry\n   671\tconventions the kernel must share. Its completion requires parity for\n   672\tvalues, errors, effects, stores, stream outcomes, and blocked states, and\n   673\tdeterministic stream/effect behavior. The pure-program tier may merge\n   674\tbefore R2 lands; R4 is complete only when both tiers are.\n   675\t\n   676\tThe phase order runs on two parallel tracks after R1: on the register\n   677\ttrack, the section 4.5 live-set change, then R2 and R4's pure-program\n   678\ttier, then R4's effects tier once B4 and R2 exist, then R3 against the\n   679\treal kernel; on the linker track, R5 together with B6, depending on R1\n   680\tand B6 only and free to land before R2 or R4.\n   681\t\n   682\t### R5: linker integration over dao.jing\n   683\t\n   684\t    New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker.cljc\n   685\t    New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_linker_test.cljc\n   686\t    Existing edits: none\n   687\t    Depends on: B6's shared linker (`yin.vm.debruijn-linker`), dao.jing,\n   688\t    dao.jing.dht, dao.jing.remote, R1 (register-hash, the validator, the\n   689\t    descriptor)\n   690\t    Must not change: B6's linker function, stack H, dao.jing, dao.jing.dht\n   691\t\n   692\tThe linker discipline is `dao.jing` over `dao.stream`, as the stack\n   693\tdesign's B6 box now specifies, and it is one function parameterized by a\n   694\tformat record. R5 is that function's second format, not a second linker.\n   695\tIt contributes the register format record\n   696\t`{:format :yin.debruijn.register :hash-fn register-hash :validate-fn ...\n   697\t:free-names-fn ...}`, the register image stored in Jing\n   698\tas its own value (the `{:bodies :instructions}` map) at its\n   699\t`segment-key`, and an R index `[R :yin.debruijn.register/address\n   700\taddress]` with the same status as B6's H index: composition data, never\n   701\tJing's. R is not the Jing address for the same reason H is not, and R5\n   702\tpins R values only, never addresses.\n   703\t\n   704\tFetch follows B6's six steps with R in place of H: the linker verifies\n   705\tthe value against its Jing address, verifies `register-hash` against R\n   706\t(the descriptor hash and contract version are inside R, so there is no\n   707\tseparate descriptor check), runs the register validator including the\n   708\tsection 4.5 live-set rules, runs the D11 and D15 closure check over\n   709\t`:load-free` operands of the validated image, and returns the verified\n   710\timage. No global loader or callback is introduced.\n   711\t\n   712\tA host without a register kernel may refuse R and instead resolve, by H,\n   713\ta stack image published for the same named root. That same-root pairing\n   714\tis the one relation in the linker that no hash checks: H and R have\n   715\tdisjoint preimages, the receiver holds neither the named datoms nor an\n   716\tH-to-R law (section 1.1 disclaims one), so a stale or swapped pairing\n   717\twould execute a different program than the R asked for with no\n   718\tdiagnostic. The rule is therefore explicit. The pairing is recorded at\n   719\tmint time beside the named root as datoms, `[root\n   720\t:yin.debruijn.code/hash H]` and `[root :yin.debruijn.register/hash R]`,\n   721\tnever as a bare H-to-R entry. Trusting it is composition trust, on the\n   722\tsame footing as the H and R indexes, and its ledger, authority, and\n   723\tprovenance are B7's name-environment work; until B7, a receiver that\n   724\tfollows the fallback is executing under that trust and must say so in its\n   725\toutcome. A receiver that requires verification does not trust the\n   726\tpairing: it fetches the named datoms by the root the pairing names,\n   727\tre-lowers them locally through `adapt` and `lower-register`, and accepts\n   728\tthe fallback only when the recomputed H and R both equal the pair it was\n   729\tgiven; otherwise it refuses with a qualified `:pairing-mismatch`. That\n   730\tre-lowering is the only check strong enough, because the datoms are the\n   731\tone artifact both hashes are functions of.\n   732\t\n   733\tNew work is the format record, the R index, the same-root pairing\n   734\tdatoms and their verification path, and tests; everything else is B6's\n   735\tfunction and Jing's guarantees. R5 depends on R1 and on B6's shared\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
