{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-23 08:10:20 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-23 15:10:20 +07 (Indochina Time)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a0bacb-3b91-7190-8412-3f1e85bb552a\n\nThe comprehensive normative R2 specification is now incorporated into [yin.vm.debruijn.register.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:366).\n\nExecutive summary: R2 remains a pure format-and-lowering phase with no dependency on the future register VM kernel. It advances the register dimension to contract version 3, defines every effect opcode and operand, generalizes verified liveness to all suspension boundaries, and freezes sparse continuation and wait-entry data contracts for R4 to interpret.\n\nNormative specification:\n\n- The canonical instruction set now includes register forms for store access, gensym, every stream operation, FFI, continuation capture, park, and resume. Alternate names such as `:stream-open`, `:stream-take`, and `:stream-write` are explicitly excluded.\n- Every opcode has an exact descriptor slot order and operand kind. An omitted gensym prefix is normalized to `\"id\"` before emission.\n- Suspension boundaries carry ascending, exact, in-band `live` vectors inside R’s hash preimage. One backward dataflow function computes and validates them.\n- Register allocation remains deterministic. Effect arguments use ordinary physical registers; results use `rd`; `:resume` is a terminator without a destination.\n- Saved continuations contain only verified live register pairs, lexical frames, sparse return frames, the suspension site, destination, resume mode, format, and R.\n- Dead register values must be discarded at suspension to prevent retention across long-lived pipelines and actor loops.\n- `register-restore` has the shared three-argument shape\n  `base entry val`. It reconstructs the sparse file and writes `val` only\n  to the recorded destination, or applies the normal return transition for\n  tail effects.\n- Terminal stream outcomes and FFI errors are classified before restoration.\n  Raw host exceptions and other non-plain resume values are refused with\n  `:resume-value`.\n- Wait entries contain only continuation data and engine transport keys.\n  The specification enumerates the transient wake keys that must be removed\n  when an FFI writer becomes a response reader, closing the previously\n  recorded stale-wake hazard.\n- R2 adds a pure `yin.vm.debruijn-register-effects` namespace for effect\n  construction, continuation construction, and validation. It introduces no\n  callback, timer, stream handle, global state, or scheduler.\n- The expanded R2 completion gate covers lowering completeness, validator\n  diagnostics, liveness exactness, stack-B4 descriptor equivalence, sparse\n  continuation structure, blocked stream and FFI paths, deterministic\n  cross-host R values, and plain-data round trips.\n- R4 remains responsible for execution parity and must reuse these\n  constructors and the shared engine seam without redefining their shapes.\n\nThe specification is ASCII-only, has no lines over 80 columns, and passes\n`git diff --check`. No implementation or test files were changed."}}
